Compiler:
	Lockfile.py:
		LockEntry:
			- stores, per named entry, its file, text hash, and code hash
			- may also store a last-change disposition, bound to the entry's spec hash (its own text combined with every currently applicable policy's text) and code hash it was recorded against

		LOCKFILE_SCHEMA_VERSION:
			- the lockfile's own schema version, bumped whenever its shape changes

		RULESET_VERSION:
			- the ruleset version every entry in a lockfile is built under together

	Parser.py:
		parse function:
			- accepts a file path
			- parses a tab-indented llmlang file into a tree of folders, files, classes, and named entries
			- a header's real name is always its first word; any words after it up to the colon are a freetext hint, discarded once the name is extracted
			- returns the parsed tree

	Extractor.py:
		extract_by_handle function:
			- accepts a file path and a handle key
			- extracts the code implementing an entry by locating its comment handle and taking everything up to the next handle
			- returns the extracted code, or nothing if the handle isn't found

	LockfileBuilder.py:
		build function:
			- accepts an llmlang file path and a lockfile output path
			- builds a lockfile by using Parser to read llmlang, Extractor to locate each entry's code, and Lockfile to record a text and code hash per entry
			- has no notion of dispositions or a change manifest at all; never reads or writes anything but the lockfile itself
			- should reproduce the same hashes when run again against an unchanged codebase
			- returns the built lock data

		finalize function:
			- accepts an llmlang file path, a lockfile output path, and a change manifest file path
			- uses Checker to find every named entry currently flagged against the existing lockfile, and refuses to write anything unless the change manifest covers every one of them with a non-empty disposition
			- accepts a fresh disposition in the manifest as a plain string, and stamps it with the entry's current spec hash and code hash
			- a spec hash combines the entry's own text with every policy currently covering it, so a disposition goes stale whether the entry's own text changed or an applicable policy did
			- for a manifest entry that's already stamped, keeps it only if both hashes still match the entry's current state; otherwise drops it as stale, along with any entry whose key no longer exists in the llmlang tree at all
			- builds every hash and disposition fresh from the current llmlang text, current code, current policies, and current manifest only; never carries anything forward from the lockfile's own prior content
			- writes both the new lockfile and the updated (stamped and pruned) manifest file, but never alters a disposition's own text
			- returns the built lock data

	LockfileChecker.py:
		check function:
			- accepts an llmlang file path and a lockfile path
			- checks a lockfile by using Parser and Extractor to compare current text and code hashes against Lockfile, reporting each entry as SPEC_DIVERGED, CODE_DIVERGED, or unchanged, alongside its file and comment handle
			- should report an entry as SPEC_DIVERGED when its own llmlang text changed, it's brand new, or an upstream policy covering it changed
			- should report an entry as CODE_DIVERGED when its handle is missing from the code or its code changed unexpectedly
			- should report an unmodified codebase as fully clean
			- returns whether everything checked out clean, and the set of named-entry tracking keys currently flagged for any reason
